2. Theory Review Questions
Answer in your own words (2–4 sentences each):

What is a transaction, and why is it important for data integrity?
Note

Your Answer

(A transaction is a group of SQL steps that run as one unit: all succeed or none do. It keeps data consistent, with no half-finished changes.)

Explain the Atomicity property using a real-world example (not from the lecture notes).
Note

Your Answer

(Atomicity: in a bank transfer, taking €100 from A and adding €100 to B must both happen. If the second step fails, the first is undone.)

What does Isolation guarantee? Why is it needed in a multi-user system?
Note

Your Answer

(Isolation means concurrent transactions don’t see each other’s unfinished work, as if they ran one after another. Without it, users would read and overwrite each other’s half-done changes.)

What is the difference between COMMIT and ROLLBACK?
Note

Your Answer

(COMMIT makes the changes permanent. ROLLBACK cancels them all.)

Explain what a SAVEPOINT is and give a scenario where it's useful.
Note

Your Answer

(A SAVEPOINT is a marker inside a transaction that you can roll back to without losing earlier work. It is useful when step 3 of 5 fails: undo only that step and continue.)

What is a dirty read? Why does PostgreSQL never allow it?
Note

Your Answer

(A dirty read is reading another transaction’s uncommitted data, which might later be rolled back. PostgreSQL never allows it because it only shows committed row versions.)

Describe the lost update problem. How can SELECT FOR UPDATE prevent it?
Note

Your Answer

(Lost update: two transactions read the same value and both write, so one overwrite is lost. SELECT FOR UPDATE locks the row, so the second transaction waits and then reads the updated value.)

What is the default isolation level in PostgreSQL? What concurrency problems does it still allow?
Note

Your Answer

(The default is READ COMMITTED. It still allows non-repeatable reads and phantom reads, and lost updates unless you lock.)

Explain MVCC in one paragraph: what is it, and what advantage does it provide?
Note

Your Answer

(MVCC (Multi-Version Concurrency Control) means PostgreSQL keeps multiple versions of each row, and each transaction sees the version from its snapshot. Readers don’t block writers and writers don’t block readers, which gives much better concurrency.)

What is a deadlock, and how does PostgreSQL handle it when one is detected?
Note

Your Answer

(A deadlock is two transactions each waiting for a lock the other holds. PostgreSQL detects it (after deadlock_timeout, 1 second by default) and aborts one transaction with an error, so the other can continue.)

