5. True/False
For each statement, write TRUE or FALSE and briefly explain why.

In PostgreSQL, a single SQL statement outside of an explicit BEGIN/COMMIT is not a transaction.FALSE, Every single statement is automatically its own transaction (autocommit).
ROLLBACK undoes all changes made since the last SAVEPOINT.FALSE. Plain ROLLBACK undoes the whole transaction. Only ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT goes back to the savepoint.
At READ COMMITTED isolation level, a transaction can never see another transaction's uncommitted changes.TRUE. Dirty reads are never allowed in PostgreSQL.
REPEATABLE READ in PostgreSQL prevents phantom reads.TRUE. PostgreSQL’s REPEATABLE READ uses a snapshot, so phantoms can’t appear.
SELECT FOR UPDATE blocks other SELECT statements on the same rows.FALSE. Normal SELECTs are not blocked (MVCC). Only other FOR UPDATE, UPDATE and DELETE statements wait.
If a deadlock occurs, PostgreSQL will wait indefinitely for it to resolve.FALSE. PostgreSQL detects the deadlock and aborts one of the transactions.
MVCC means PostgreSQL keeps multiple versions of each row, allowing readers and writers to work concurrently without blocking.TRUE.
Once a transaction is committed, its changes can be undone with ROLLBACK.FALSE. After COMMIT, changes are permanent. ROLLBACK can’t undo them.
